## Event, participation and entering law

Argued February 23, 2000; decided May 1, 2000; certiorari to the Fifth Circuit, 158 F.3d 241, No. 97-20796, whose lower caption is Moreau v. Harris County. This is distinct from the earlier Moreau v. Klevenhagen precedent. The finality basis is disposition of all nonabandoned claims, not party consent to jurisdiction. All nine participated at argument and decision; no disqualification is supplied. Quorum is met and five votes suffice. Research cutoff: May 1, 2000. Uncoordinated May 1 events in the next chunk supply no entering law; later entry of an earlier-alphabetical same-date Record reopens no assignment.

The class challenged a policy requiring paid absence charged against valid accrued compensatory time. It did not involve unpaid leave or simple forfeiture. Bureau commanders set balance limits according to staffing needs; supervisors could direct reduction after employees reached them. The district declaration and dependent $21,360 fee award were displaced by final judgment for all defendants. Wrongful-refusal and retaliation theories were abandoned and are not revived. No controlling agreement term was identified; that is not proof that agreements do not exist or all employees consented to forced use.

The frozen sources preserve the complete section 207(o), implementing grant, sections 553.23, 553.25 and 553.27, their separate qualifications and Moreau's actual cohort/representative rules. Auer supplies responsible, fair regulatory interpretation within text; NationsBank, Smiley, Boston & Maine, and current FDA/U.S. West require actual authority, actual agency grounds and statutory fit. No modern deference doctrine enters. The original September 14, 1992 letter is unrecovered; the published recitation establishes its issuer, date, advisory status and specific-consent position. No unprovided qualification, Government-brief endorsement, signature instrument or internal delegation is invented.

## Stone

The approved October 31 position requires affirmance: section 207(o) creates no employee veto over compelled paid use, the letter has persuasion-based rather than controlling statutory weight, and Auer permits no new condition contrary to an unambiguous regulation. Stone joins all three parts while preserving protected requests, accrued value and binding agreements. Those limits are shared and operative, not optional caveats. No general employer-control presumption or confiscation authority is adopted. No standing fallback supplies a substantive ground or author eligibility.

## Form, assignment and final compatibility

The separate pre-count form receipt fixes one signed lead with three legally distinct parts and the necessary separate writings. The Chief assigns. Stone-Zsela, O'Connor, Kennedy, Souter and Thomas are eligible for the whole opinion. Scalia supports the statute and Auer application but not Part II's statutory-deference ground; judgment agreement does not make him eligible to carry it. Multiple eligible Justices can carry the whole opinion, so divided delivery is unwarranted.

**Fresh counts before assignment:** Stone-Zsela 6; Stevens 5; O'Connor 6; Scalia 6; Kennedy 6; Souter 6; Thomas 6; Ginsburg 7; Breyer 6. Last counted event: Slack v. McDaniel, 2000-04-26; Ginsburg.

The eligible authors and balance pool are the same five Justices: Stone-Zsela, O'Connor, Kennedy, Souter and Thomas, each with six counted leads. The Chief assigns the whole opinion to Kennedy. The work is coordinating three distinct inquiries: the statute's mandatory request protection and optional scheduling terms, this letter's actual authority, and interpretation within the regulation's text. Kennedy's prior Auer and Boston & Maine opinions supply a concrete fit for separating a responsible agency's actual interpretation from judicially added authority or grounds; his frozen commitment also preserves the statutory protections without a general employer-control presumption. This is fit among settled commitments, not expansion of a coalition or a historical-authorship import. Stone is an equally counted eligible alternative whose approved position separates those same inquiries, but no additional accommodation or special advantage of self-authorship is established. Kennedy can carry the agreed limits without changing any frozen proposition. All other eligible pool members remain capable; authorship does not alter the six/five/six joins.

Final joins: six in Parts I and III; five in Part II. Souter's future-regulation reservation is retained. Scalia may reject the letter's construction even assuming the authority it claims, without finding the missing endorsement fact. The three opposing Associates adopt consent-based agreement proceedings, not automatic restoration of the district declaration. Their shared dissent does not announce that the employees' construction is the only possible judicial reading; Breyer's further explanation preserves persuasive sufficiency and conditional authoritative reasonableness. Ginsburg joins that bounded explanation, not a necessary holding that this letter conclusively receives Chevron weight. No historical part join is automatically imported. The reconciliation's express proposition-level clarification controls these joins; no frozen input is rewritten.

