Five Associates—O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter and Thomas—support the statutory default and the independently modeled conclusion that Auer cannot supply the condition contrary to the regulation's permissive text. Four—O'Connor, Kennedy, Souter and Thomas—support the letter-specific no-Chevron/Skidmore ground. Three—Stevens, Ginsburg and Breyer—support the consent-based agreement remand, with their sufficient routes distinguished above. No numerical coalition here includes a replacement Chief vote. No historical five-Justice Part III automatically becomes a present controlling rationale.

Scalia's historical reliance on the Government brief's representation of the Secretary's position is not an additional neutral fact in this handoff. That brief was not supplied to the clean model. His rejection may proceed on the assumption most favorable to authoritative status; it does not require finding an unproved signature, delegation or endorsement. Breyer's persuasive alternative likewise prevents an unproved document-status fact from becoming an indispensable premise. Neither rationale invents the full letter's contents or decides a hypothetical agency-authorization dispute. Further factual reliance on an unsupplied brief or internal instrument requires fresh neutral review, not a reconciliation insertion.

#### Operative rules and preserved distinctions

The five affirming Associates hold that section 207(o)(5) protects an employee's request for use within a reasonable period absent undue disruption; it does not make requests the exclusive method of consuming validly accrued time. Paragraph (o)(2)'s agreement condition governs time substituted for cash. Neither provision categorically requires a separate term authorizing compulsory paid use. Actual lawful restrictions in an agreement remain enforceable at their proper scope; the absence of an identified controlling provision is not a finding that no agreements exist or every employee consented. No general employer-control presumption is adopted.

The four-Associate deference ground confines this advisory letter to persuasive consideration because its supported status lacks force of law. It does not disregard its reasoning by label, make every informal agency statement irrelevant, or replace Auer's own-regulation inquiry with Chevron's statutory one. The five-Associate regulatory ground reads section 553.23(a)(2)'s permission for additional agreement terms as permission, not a requirement that every compelled-use policy be expressly authorized. Auer remains controlling for a responsible agency's fair and considered interpretation within the text; an unambiguous permission cannot be amended by interpretation. Souter's future-regulation reservation is express: no conclusion here establishes that the Secretary lacks power to adopt a valid new rule limiting forced use.

The three opposing Associates treat the statutory arrangement as a consensual exception to ordinary cash overtime, with the agreement governing compulsory consumption. Cash redemption and paid absence are different transactions; a cashout power does not itself establish forced-use authority. Their remand applies the actual earlier-hire practice and later-hire agreement routes. It does not require negotiation of every optional provision, identical individual agreements, or a general collective-bargaining right.

All detailed operative qualifications in the frozen commitments remain: time-and-one-half accrual; applicable 480/240-hour caps and cash compensation for additional overtime at the cap; cashout and distinct termination-rate rules; knowing voluntary agreement formation and the preserved transition route; realistic good-faith availability; reasonable-period request protection; the unreasonable public-service burden required for undue disruption, with mere inconvenience insufficient; separate other-time exclusions and the unrelated court-reporter conditions. No independent violation is found. Moreau's cohort distinction is not erased by shorthand about consent. No constitutional property question or general confiscation power is resolved.

#### Remedy and departure assessment

The five affirming Associates leave the Fifth Circuit's judgment for all defendants in force against the categorical FLSA challenge. The employees' declaration and dependent $21,360 fee award remain displaced. There is no agreement-specific remand merely because an unidentified term might exist. The three opposing Associates would reverse or vacate that defense judgment and remand for actual agreement coverage, terms and validity; they would not automatically reinstate the declaration or fee award. Neither route orders negotiations, a mandatory trial, new damages or a fee amount, or revives abandoned wrongful-refusal and retaliation claims. The reviewed finality determination rests on disposition of nonabandoned claims, not party consent to jurisdiction.

No Associate judgment departure is established. The historical generic agreement premise is confined here to the frozen lower-record distinction between an unidentified controlling term and proof that no agreement exists. That supported distinction explains the explicit remedy limits; it does not generate a new liability finding. Souter's reservation and Breyer's persuasive alternative are express historical confirmations/clarifications, not changes to frozen bytes. Scalia's separate present assent to the Auer proposition is identified rather than falsely described as a historical Part III join; it changes no historical position on a contrary proposition and never supplies assent to the statutory-deference rule he rejects.

There is no unresolved reconciliation blocker on the supported paths. Assembly must preserve proposition-level joins, the letter-source limit and the actual-agreement/remedy limits. It must determine authorship and any additional joins from its separately authorized inputs.

### Christensen reconciliation — precise separate-writing joins

This supplements, without changing any byte of, CHRISTENSEN_COMMITMENTS or CHRISTENSEN_RECONCILED. It answers the assembly join-scope question using only the already reviewed legal propositions. It adds no fact, current-law premise, vote or author assignment. The reconciled reference to Ginsburg's historical joins in both dissents is historical evidence, not an automatic present join.

**Consent explanation:** Stevens and Ginsburg independently support the consent-based statutory interpretation: the conditioned alternative to cash overtime requires an applicable agreement authorizing compulsory use, with Moreau's actual earlier-hire and later-hire routes preserved. Breyer supports its statutory reasons as making the consent interpretation reasonable and persuasive, and joins the consent-rule/remand conclusion on the independently sufficient persuasive-agency route. He may join a common explanation written at that shared level. He is not committed to a further statement that the statute unambiguously requires this exclusive interpretation regardless of agency weight, or that the County's reading is not a possible judicial construction. If a writing contains that stronger proposition, his join must exclude it.

**Agency explanation:** Breyer supports an explanation that the responsible agency's consent construction is reasonable within the actual implementing delegation, and independently persuasive under Skidmore even if controlling statutory deference is unavailable. Ginsburg may join that complete bounded explanation, including the conditional proposition that the construction is reasonable if authoritative Chevron treatment applies. Her independently sufficient statutory ground does not prevent that agreement. Her join does not establish that the letter conclusively qualifies for Chevron, settle every agency form question, or adopt an unsupported government-brief/Secretary-endorsement fact. A writing instead holding this letter necessarily authoritative must limit her join to its Skidmore, reasonableness and remand portions.

Stevens likewise preserves Skidmore and may accept the bounded reasonableness/conditional-deference explanation; the independent freeze does not require his formal join in a separate agency writing. A historical footnote's agreement is not an assignment or formal join.

Scalia's separate function remains different: he refuses a form-based categorical denial of authoritative statutory consideration but rejects this construction even assuming the claimed authority. His agreement in the judgment supplies no join in the four-Associate no-Chevron rule. Souter's separate function, if expressed separately, is solely the future-regulation reservation already stated in the reconciled handoff; it does not endorse the present letter or require a new rulemaking.

Assembly can use a shared dissent explanation plus a bounded agency explanation, or partial joins, provided these propositions and exclusions remain express. No present author or historical writing structure is prescribed.

